LeetCode 687 — Longest Univalue Path
1. Problem Statement

You are given the root of a binary tree.

You need to find the length of the longest path where every node in the path has the same value.

The path can start and end at any node.
It does not have to pass through the root.
The answer is measured in number of edges, not number of nodes.

Example:

        5
       / \
      4   5
     / \   \
    1   1   5

The longest same-value path is:

5 → 5 → 5

Number of edges:

2
2. Intuition

At every node, we ask:

How far can I extend a same-value path through my left child? How far can I extend it through my right child?

For example:

        4
       / \
      4   4
     /     \
    4       4

At the root:

Left side can contribute some same-value edges.
Right side can contribute some same-value edges.

So the longest path passing through the current node is:

leftPath + rightPath

We update:

gmax = Math.max(gmax, leftPath + rightPath);

But when returning to the parent, we cannot return both sides.

Why?

Because this:

left → root → right

is already a complete path. We cannot extend both branches upward.

So we return only:

Math.max(leftPath, rightPath)



Logic :

I solve this problem using DFS and recursion. For every node, I recursively calculate the longest univalue path that can be extended from its left and right subtrees.
Then I check whether the current node has the same value as its left child. If they are equal, I extend the left path by one edge. Otherwise, the left contribution becomes zero. I do the same for the right child.
At each node, both valid left and right paths can be combined to form a path passing through the current node. Therefore, I update a global maximum using leftPath + rightPath.
However, when returning the result to the parent, I can only return one side because a path extending upward cannot branch in two directions. So I return the maximum of the left and right paths.


Code :

class Solution {
    int gmax=0;
    public int longestUnivaluePath(TreeNode root) {
        longestUnivaluePath1(root);
        return gmax;
    }
    public int longestUnivaluePath1(TreeNode root){
        if(root==null){
            return 0;
        }
        int left=longestUnivaluePath1(root.left);
        int right=longestUnivaluePath1(root.right);
        int leftpath=0;
        int rightpath=0;
        if(root.left!=null && root.val==root.left.val){
            leftpath=left+1;
        }
        if(root.right!=null && root.val==root.right.val){
            rightpath=right+1;
            
        }
        gmax=Math.max(gmax,leftpath+rightpath);
        return Math.max(leftpath,rightpath);
    }
}
